WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
[Image of the seal of the U.S. Public Health
Service, 1798]

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:16.760
[Federal Security Agency Public Health Service
and American Diabetes Association present]

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:25.900
[The Story of Wendy Hill]

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:32.740
[A Warner News Inc. Production, Produced by...
Alfred Butterfield, 

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.900
Directed by... Joseph E. Henabery, Photographed by... William O. Steiner, A. S. C.]

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.580
[Doctor Woods:] This is Greendale. A friendly
neighborly town. Maybe sort of an old-fashioned

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:53.590
town. Oh, we've grown through the years in Greendale,
but still we've kept in touch with old friends.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:02.140
I suppose I know our people pretty well. You're
bound to in my profession, and there are ever

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.430
so many interesting human stories to tell
about them, but I wonder if you wouldn't like

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:10.979
to hear the story that I think is the most
important one I could tell.

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:15.719
It begins just across the street from me, over
in the offices of Greendale's leading law firm.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.450
Now we're not particularly interested in Bates
&amp; Bates, except that the girl I want to tell

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:27.350
you about has been pleasantly employed there
ever since she graduated from high school.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.990
Wendy Jameson, I mean Wendy Hill, I'm always
forgetting to use her married name. You

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.390
see, she had just been married at the time our story begins.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:42.640
Well, Wendy Hill was about as fine and wholesome
a girl as you'd find anywhere, and everything

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.100
had been working out right for Wendy lately.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.360
Of course she had no way of knowing that this
would be the day that would change the whole

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.550
course of her life.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:02:06.790
[Wendy attempts to cross the street and is
hit by a truck. She screams.]

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:14.080
When I heard the scream, I moved fast. You
can imagine how I felt when I saw that the

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:22.220
victim was Wendy. Our town knows how to handle
emergencies.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.360
Wendy hadn't regained consciousness yet and,
of course, I went over to the hospital with her.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.380
It took only about half an hour to patch Wendy's
bruises and send her upstairs to a quiet room.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.140
Her husband, Jim, had come over.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.440
Wendy wasn't seriously hurt thank goodness,
but she was still suffering from considerable shock.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.720
I just dropped in on her for a minute
in her room.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.080
Well, here you are. Hello, Jim.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.140
[Jim:] Hello, doctor.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.680
[Doctor Woods:] Hello, Wendy. Well, young
lady, look what you've got yourself into this time.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:07.240
[Wendy:] I hope you're not going to keep me here
very long. I couldn't stand that. Oh, oh, it hurts.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.740
[Jim:] Is she all right, Dr. Woods?

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:15.060
[Doctor Woods:] Of course she is; don't worry
about her. And don't you worry either, little lady.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.140
Just take it easy and we'll have you out of
here in no time at all.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.180
But next morning I was almost forced to
swallow those cheerful words. I had just finished

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.480
dictating to Miss Malone when the phone rang.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:36.590
It was the hospital. Nurse Henderson wanted
to give me the results of routine tests on

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:41.930
Wendy. What she said was surprising and not
so good.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:47.240
Wendy's urinalysis showed sugar. Of course,
I asked for confirmation. Nurse Henderson

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.970
said she'd make a blood sugar test immediately.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:56.190
Sugar. That points straight at diabetes. It
might be a false alarm, but still I thought

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.870
I'd get in touch with Jim.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.200
[Doctor Woods:] Miss Malone, will you see
if you can get Mr. Hill on the phone right away?

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:03.980
[Miss Malone:] Yes, sir.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:04.720
[Doctor Woods:] Thanks.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.280
[Miss Malone:] I have Mr. Hill, Doctor Woods.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:12.760
[Doctor Woods:] Thanks.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:22.640
Hello, Jim? Say, Jim, I wonder if you could
knock off for just half an hour or so and

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:27.020
come over to my office? No, nothing to get
upset about.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:37.320
No, I'd rather talk to you here. Fine, Jim.
No, don't rush. Just come as soon as you can.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.780
Oh and Jim, do you think you could stop by home
and bring your service discharge papers with

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:49.180
you? Fine. Thanks. Good-bye.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:57.250
While Jim was on his way to my office, Nurse
Henderson over at the hospital was starting

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.930
Wendy's blood sugar test.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.540
[Miss Malone:] Hello, Mr. Hill, the doctor
will be expecting you. Come in.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.140
[Doctor Woods:] Hello, Jim.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.100
[Jim:] Hello, Dr. Woods.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.340
[Doctor Woods:] Glad to see you. Sit down.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.680
[Jim:] Has something gone wrong with Wendy?

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:24.900
[Doctor Woods:] Well, just a second, Jim,
I'll be right with you.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:37.880
Hello? Yes? Yes, Miss Henderson. 275. Yes,
I guess that's it. Much obliged.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:52.400
Now, Jim, I don't want you to be alarmed.
This has nothing to do with Wendy's injuries,

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:58.670
thank goodness, but a routine urinalysis showed
sugar in the urine and that's 

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.400
a tell-tale sign of diabetes.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:07.380
[Jim:] Diabetes, Wendy? It's impossible. Couldn't
they have gotten it wrong?

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.660
[Doctor Woods:] That's why I had the blood
sugar test made for confirmation. That was

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.210
the hospital on the phone just now.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.970
[Jim:] Are you going to tell Wendy?

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.050
[Doctor Woods:] Of course. She has to know.
She has to cooperate with us.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:26.380
[Jim:] I just can't believe this about Wendy.
She seems so well. Good appetite, plenty of pep and all.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.620
[Doctor Woods:] Well, then you can thank your
lucky stars she was knocked down by that truck yesterday.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:31.460
[Jim:] I don't get it.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.680
[Doctor Woods:] Well, if it hadn't been for
that accident, I don't think she would have

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.210
had a urinalysis for months. You look okay,
Jim.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:38.449
[Jim:] Oh, sure.

00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:42.109
[Doctor Woods:] Did you bring your service
discharge papers with you?

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.060
[Jim:] Yes, here they are.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.400
[Doctor Woods:] Thanks.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.380
That's good.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.560
[Jim:] As a matter of fact, I just took out
some new insurance last week. I passed the

00:06:56.699 --> 00:06:57.620
exam just fine.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.300
[Doctor Woods:] And that's even better. We'll
drop in on Wendy right now .

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.380
[Wendy:] Hello, sweetheart.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.960
[Jim:] Hello, darling.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.920
[Wendy:] Jim, something is wrong. I can tell
by the look on your face.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.480
What is it, doctor?

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.800
[Doctor Woods:] Well, we made routine tests
of your urine and blood here in the hospital

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.949
and both tests show sugar.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.760
[Wendy:] Sugar?

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:41.840
But that means...?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.440
[Doctor Woods:] Yes, diabetes.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:47.700
[Wendy:] Oh, Jim!

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:48.660
[Jim:] [There there, dear.]

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.200
[Wendy:] Oh, Jim. What are we going to do?
I wish we'd never been married.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.100
[Doctor Woods:] Wait a minute, Wendy. I thought
you were a sensible girl.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:03.550
[Wendy:] Dr. Woods, you don't understand.
We wanted children. I know what diabetes means.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:10.050
I know a girl who has it. They told her...well, they said she could never have a baby.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:16.800
[Doctor Woods:] Listen to me, Wendy, you and
Jim certainly can have children. The chance

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:23.020
of diabetes affecting your children is small.
We won't worry about it now.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:27.340
[Wendy:] But I, but I feel so afraid.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:34.029
[Doctor Woods:] Look here. It's a fact that
heredity is important in diabetes.

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:38.320
Children in diabetic families have a far greater
chance of winding up with the disease than

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:44.229
those in families without it, but the chance
is well worth running unless both parents

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:45.889
have the disease.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.140
Now, as for you, we know about it soon enough
to bring you under good control. You can live

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:58.660
in comfort and security and you certainly
can have children, if you cooperate in your treatment.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.020
[Wendy:] I'll try. Oh, my arm hurts.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:07.700
[Doctor Woods:] Of course it does, but you
know, Wendy, that accident was probably the

00:09:07.709 --> 00:09:13.290
luckiest thing that ever happened to you.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:17.999
Strange how surprised I'd been to find diabetes
in Wendy. Hardly any symptoms that might have

00:09:17.999 --> 00:09:23.119
warned me, and yet that's often the case, as
20 years practice here in Greendale has certainly

00:09:23.119 --> 00:09:26.860
taught me.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.439
Jake Wilson, for example. I never knew Jake
was deliberately concealing his symptoms.

00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:36.199
He let things go so long that Dr. van Voorhies
and I had to take part of Jake's right leg

00:09:36.199 --> 00:09:41.259
three years ago.

00:09:41.259 --> 00:09:47.329
And Tommy Higgins. Just couldn't seem to get
a hit anymore. It was breaking his heart.

00:09:47.329 --> 00:09:52.610
Diabetes often affects eyesight. Tommy's under
good control now.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:57.829
And Emma Thorndike. Anybody could have seen she
was a set-up for diabetes. It strikes much

00:09:57.829 --> 00:10:03.969
oftener among fat people. Emma really hated
giving up those chocolates, but it saved her life.

00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:13.269
It's amazing how suddenly diabetes seems to
begin in children. On Thursday, her mother told

00:10:13.269 --> 00:10:16.499
me little Janey Barrett began complaining
about being tired.

00:10:16.499 --> 00:10:22.170
She started asking for water every few minutes;
a danger signal. Luckily Mrs. Barrett brought

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:29.170
her to me right away. Janey's on insulin
now and doing well.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:40.670
Shortly after sending Wendy home I stopped
in to see her; it was on 
a Saturday.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:45.220
Hello, Jim.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.200
[Jim:] Hello, doctor.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:47.360
[Doctor Woods:] Hello, Wendy.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:48.760
[Wendy:] Hello, Dr. Woods.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:50.440
[Doctor Woods:] Jim, how is our patient today?

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.249
[Jim:] She looks okay to me, doctor, but man,
oh, man, is she ever a hard one to get along with.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:03.460
[Wendy:] Don't you believe it, doctor. This
person just won't follow orders in regard to housework.

00:11:03.559 --> 00:11:09.959
[Jim:] Oh, I'll get even with you. Excuse
me, doctor, I've got Wendy's lunch on the stove.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.020
[Doctor Woods:] Go right ahead, Jim.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.320
[Wendy:] Put your coat over there, doctor.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:23.640
[Doctor Woods:] Thanks, Wendy. Now, young
lady, tell me just how do you feel?

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:30.920
[Wendy:] Well, I'm still a little sore and
stiff but otherwise I'm fine and Jim has been so considerate.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.660
[Jim:] It's ready.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.620
[Doctor Woods:] [Laughter]

00:11:34.629 --> 00:11:37.800
[Jim:] Come and get it. I'll bring it in.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:44.800
[Wendy:] Oh, you've saved my life. I'm starving.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:52.860
[Doctor Woods:] [Laughter] You've got the
right idea, Jim, but you might let her have

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.209
just a little bit more.

00:11:55.209 --> 00:11:57.309
[Jim:] Okay, doctor.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:03.491
[Wendy:] I should say so. I guess I just love
eating. What's wrong with that anyhow?

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.149
It wasn't making me fat.

00:12:05.149 --> 00:12:12.269
[Doctor Woods:] No, but it was making your
diabetes worse.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:17.840
You see, a normal person has plenty of insulin to handle a lot of sugars and starches, 

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.639
but your trouble is that you don't have enough insulin.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:25.410
[Jim:] So it stands to reason you have to
go light on the starchy stuff.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:31.809
[Doctor Woods:] That's right, Jim. Insulin
is necessary for the proper use of starches

00:12:31.809 --> 00:12:33.069
and sugars.

00:12:33.069 --> 00:12:38.889
Now, with diabetic people, careful diet and
other precautions are a great help, but when

00:12:38.889 --> 00:12:42.879
they're not enough we can, thank goodness,
call on outside help.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:51.260
And here it is in this little box. Insulin,
one of the greatest discoveries in medical history.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:59.119
We're starting you off on insulin, Wendy.
I don't know whether you'll stay on it or not.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:06.700
Most people do. Your life and happiness are
secure as long as we have insulin to give you.

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:14.449
You look pretty good. Well, you better be
getting to your lunch and I better be getting

00:13:14.449 --> 00:13:18.989
to my other patients.

00:13:18.989 --> 00:13:22.989
Wendy was getting along even better than I
had expected. She had gone back to work just

00:13:22.989 --> 00:13:24.339
a week ago.

00:13:24.339 --> 00:13:30.569
[Man:] Thank you. Say, you weren't here the
last time I came in.

00:13:30.569 --> 00:13:34.189
[Wendy:] No, I was home for a few days.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:40.860
[Man:] Oh, that's right. My wife was telling
me. You had diabetes, wasn't it?

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:42.819
[Wendy:] Well, I still have it I guess.

00:13:42.819 --> 00:13:48.319
[Man:] Well, I'm glad I came in. You didn't
know my younger sister, Kelly, did you?

00:13:48.319 --> 00:13:49.980
[Wendy:] No, I don't believe I did.

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:54.749
[Man:] A couple of years ago they told her
she had diabetes, but she didn't pay any attention

00:13:54.749 --> 00:13:57.469
to what those doctors told her.

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:58.760
[Wendy:] Oh, she didn't?

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:04.559
[Man:] No, and she got it all right. She just
took Witherspoon's Golden Remedy every morning

00:14:04.559 --> 00:14:06.819
and night right up to the day she died.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:12.960
[Doctor Woods:] Wendy seemed to be getting
a lot of free advice. At first, it didn't bother her.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:17.760
[Mrs. Barton:] Why, Wendy...

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:22.899
[Wendy:] Why, Mrs. Barton. How are you? You
never did come to see our little place.

00:14:22.899 --> 00:14:24.309
[Mrs. Barton:] Yes, I know.

00:14:24.309 --> 00:14:28.549
[Mrs. Barton:] But I heard about your trouble,
my dear, and I didn't want to be getting in

00:14:28.549 --> 00:14:33.989
the way at a time like this. Diabetes is such
a terrible thing.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.920
[Wendy:] Oh, I'm getting along just fine,
Mrs. Barton.

00:14:37.929 --> 00:14:46.909
[Mrs. Barton:] Yes, I know but...you never
can tell. Now, Mrs. Cameron, she just kept

00:14:47.079 --> 00:14:52.939
right on going downhill. I don't know how
many people I've known that died --

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:55.119
[Wendy:] Dr. Woods says there's nothing to
worry about.

00:14:55.119 --> 00:15:00.739
[Mrs. Barton:] Well, I hope he's right, but...
Well, good-bye, my dear.

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:02.700
[Wendy:] Good-bye.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.300
[Doctor Woods:] Poor Wendy was becoming the
target for too much uninformed talk, especially

00:15:12.559 --> 00:15:17.979
her Great Aunt Henrietta's. That old lady
was inspecting Wendy's new home for the first time.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:25.100
[Aunt Henrietta opens a dresser drawer and sees a baby bootie.]

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.980
[Great Aunt Henrietta:] You're going to have
a baby?

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.119
[Wendy:] Well, I hope so.

00:15:35.119 --> 00:15:48.099
[Great Aunt Henrietta:] Sit down, Wendy, I
want to talk to you. You can't have a baby with diabetes.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:48.940
[Wendy:] Oh, but I --

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:52.679
[Great Aunt Henrietta:] -- don't you know
you can't have a baby?

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:59.680
[Doctor Woods:] Worried, unhappy and confused,
Wendy and her husband, Jim, came to my office

00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:04.920
the next afternoon. I guess I laid down the
law to them pretty hard.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:08.189
Nothing gets me madder than loose medical
talk by people who don't know what they're

00:16:08.189 --> 00:16:14.910
talking about. It's dangerous. I think I cleared
things up pretty well with Wendy and Jim and

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:16.649
they are a sensible pair.

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:20.089
[Music]

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:24.520
[Door with sign that reads, "Delivery Rooom."]

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:29.859
Well, the rest of the story goes pretty fast,
and happily. Jim was nervous in the waiting

00:16:29.859 --> 00:16:36.859
room, but it was my pleasure to announce to
him that he was the father of a fine new baby boy.

00:16:39.089 --> 00:16:46.089
My, were they proud on the day they brought
the infant, Jimmy, home for the first time.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:58.220
As Jimmy grew older, Wendy and Jim would have
faced anything together for that little child.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:02.280
Jimmy's second birthday rolled around.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:07.100
There have been a couple of others since.
Big Jim was always bringing something home

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:09.270
for that youngster, seems to me.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:14.000
I made one call on the family back during
the chicken pox season when Jimmy was three.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:19.900
Now that he's a big grown-up four, he and
Wendy just drop by occasionally for a checkup.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:26.900
What a sturdy young fellow that Jimmy is.
A nice kid too.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:34.210
[ Doctor gives Jimmy a lollipop. ]

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:40.320
Well, that's about all there is to the story
I wanted to tell you. The story of Wendy Hill.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:46.130
Oh, yes, Wendy is going to have another baby.
Her husband, Jim, doesn't know it yet. He'll

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:52.010
know before very long though. He's meeting
Wendy and Jimmy downstairs.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:58.820
Yes, I guess the worst thing about trouble
is running away from it, letting fear keep

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:03.920
you from doing what you should do. Wendy Hill
knows that today.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:08.570
There's no earthly reason, now that we have
insulin, why people with diabetes, if they

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:14.880
act soon enough, can't live long and happy
lives. Lives where they can sometimes do some

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.550
good for other people too.

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:20.600
[ Background Music ]

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.860
[Doctor looks at clock.]

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.840
Miss Malone, I guess it's just about time
for me to have my own insulin.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:32.860
[ Background Music ]

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:45.860
[The End]

